# Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)

## Purpose

Define which node labels an authenticated user can query through the Cypher
proxy described in `overview.md`. The graph below stores authorization metadata;
the proxy must enforce these rules before executing a query.

## Graph model

```text
(:User)-[:HAS_ROLE]->(:Role)-[:HAS_ACCESS_OF_NODE]->(:AccessNode)
```

### Nodes

| Label | Properties | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `User` | `name` | A user identified by a unique, stable authenticated username. |
| `Role` | `name`, `privileges` | A named set of permissions shared by users. |
| `AccessNode` | `name` | An access target representing one exact data-node label, such as `Customer`. |

Example properties:

```json
{"User": {"name": "analyst_01"}}
{"Role": {"name": "customer_reader", "privileges": ["readOnly"]}}
{"AccessNode": {"name": "Customer"}}
```

`AccessNode` is a policy node, not a business-data node. Its `name` must match the
target label exactly, including case. User names, role names, and access-target
names must each be unique within their respective node label.

### Relationships

| Relationship | Direction | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `HAS_ROLE` | `User` → `Role` | Assigns a role to a user. A user may have multiple roles. |
| `HAS_ACCESS_OF_NODE` | `Role` → `AccessNode` | Grants the role access to the target label. A role may have multiple targets. |

## Privileges

Use the property name `privileges` consistently (correcting `privilages`).

| Value | Intended meaning | Current proxy behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `readOnly` | Read the labels linked to the role. | Permit only reads that pass the full validation policy. |
| `full` | Read and write the labels linked to the role. | Writes remain rejected by the current read-only proxy. |

These values are alternatives: use one per role, such as `["readOnly"]`.
`full` does not grant access to every label, administrative commands, or policy
metadata. Write support requires a separate validation design before activation.

Privileges apply to every access target linked to the role. If one user needs
different privileges for different labels, assign separate roles.

## Authorization rules

1. Resolve the user from trusted authentication context, never from a username
   supplied inside the query.
2. Collect grants through `User → HAS_ROLE → Role → HAS_ACCESS_OF_NODE → AccessNode`.
   Grants from multiple roles are additive; this model has no explicit deny rules.
3. Deny access when no applicable grant exists. Missing users, missing roles,
   empty privileges, and unknown privilege values must not produce access.
4. Validate the complete parsed query, including every subquery and `UNION`
   branch. Every possible data access must be covered by an applicable grant.
5. Initially reject patterns whose label scope cannot be proven, including
   unlabeled scans and dynamic label access. For multi-label nodes, require
   authorization for all labels; a permitted label must not expose a node that
   also carries a restricted label.
6. Prevent query access to RBAC metadata and prevent users from changing their
   own role assignments or grants. Manage policy through a trusted admin path.
7. Apply the read-only syntax policy, `EXPLAIN` check, execution timeout, and
   result limits described in `overview.md`. A label grant never bypasses them.

## Example

```text
(analyst_01:User)
    └─ HAS_ROLE → (customer_reader:Role {privileges: ["readOnly"]})
                      └─ HAS_ACCESS_OF_NODE → (:AccessNode {name: "Customer"})
```

This user may read authorized `Customer` nodes through the validated proxy.
The role grants no access to `Order` nodes. A query that also accesses `Order`
requires another applicable grant, and write queries remain rejected.

## Scope and open decisions

This draft defines label-level grants only. It does not yet define permissions
for relationship types, individual properties, rows, or separate databases.
Before enabling relationship traversal, path returns, or whole-node returns,
define how those operations avoid exposing restricted nodes, relationships,
and properties. Reject unsupported access forms until their checks are defined.
